WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.200
Why does this matter?

2
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.812
Once the servers are under Arc, an assessor
asks questions a script can answer.

3
00:00:05.812 --> 00:00:09.562
Which servers lack the monitoring
and endpoint protection agents?

4
00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:12.312
Which still reach Azure
over the public internet?

5
00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:17.000
Which run an operating system or SQL
Server version past the end of support?

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.300
Is each SQL Server instance's
host still connected?

7
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.438
And which policy assignments are failing,
and which aren't enforcing anything?

8
00:00:24.438 --> 00:00:29.874
The validation kit already has seven
Arc-track checks on Resource Graph: tags,

9
00:00:29.874 --> 00:00:33.356
agent status, baseline
compliance, CMDB coverage,

10
00:00:33.356 --> 00:00:37.688
Update Manager, role assignments,
and two tools on one setting.

11
00:00:37.688 --> 00:00:39.538
This module doesn't repeat them.

12
00:00:39.538 --> 00:00:41.338
Its checks read other records.

13
00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:43.758
One more thing frames the module:

14
00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:49.405
the workbook plans on Windows Server
2016 being gone from production by its

15
00:00:49.405 --> 00:00:54.874
January twelfth, 2027 end of support,
with Windows Server 2019 the minimum.

16
00:00:54.874 --> 00:00:59.440
That's the program's planning
assumption, not a federal requirement,

17
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.760
and any 2016 server that
remains is an exception.
